WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.310
Binary protocol safety depends on continuous validation work.

2
00:00:03.310 --> 00:00:11.170
Unit tests should cover valid messages, minimum and maximum sizes, boundary values, optional fields, missing fields, truncated messages, oversized

3
00:00:11.170 --> 00:00:16.135
fields, invalid versions, invalid state transitions, authentication failures, and resource-limit decisions.

4
00:00:16.135 --> 00:00:19.031
These tests make the protocol contract executable.

5
00:00:19.031 --> 00:00:22.341
Regression tests are especially valuable for parser fixes.

6
00:00:22.341 --> 00:00:32.683
When a bug is found, the team should add a test that captures the expected safe behavior so the same assumption does not return later.

7
00:00:32.683 --> 00:00:42.199
Boundary cases should include lengths around limits, offsets near frame edges, counts of zero and one, maximum supported counts, and nested data at

8
00:00:42.199 --> 00:00:43.026
allowed depth.

9
00:00:43.026 --> 00:00:49.232
Fuzzing has a defensive role: it exercises parser robustness and memory safety under varied inputs.

10
00:00:49.232 --> 00:00:57.093
It should be integrated as a way to find crashes, sanitizer findings, hangs, unexpected allocations, and inconsistent error handling.

11
00:00:57.093 --> 00:01:03.712
AddressSanitizer, UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer, compiler warnings, static analysis, and coverage-guided testing can all strengthen confidence when they

12
00:01:03.712 --> 00:01:05.367
fit the build system.

13
00:01:05.367 --> 00:01:07.022
Observability completes the loop.

14
00:01:07.022 --> 00:01:14.469
Production systems should expose safe counters for rejected messages, parse error categories, version mismatches, resource-limit rejections, failed

15
00:01:14.469 --> 00:01:17.365
authentication or integrity checks, and unusual rates.

16
00:01:17.365 --> 00:01:23.570
These signals help teams spot compatibility problems, client rollouts, corrupted data sources, or operational pressure.

17
00:01:23.570 --> 00:01:25.639
Review questions should be concrete.

18
00:01:25.639 --> 00:01:27.294
Where are lengths checked?

19
00:01:27.294 --> 00:01:28.535
Are offsets bounded?

20
00:01:28.535 --> 00:01:30.190
What state is required?

21
00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:31.845
Which versions are accepted?

22
00:01:31.845 --> 00:01:33.913
When is a message trusted?

23
00:01:33.913 --> 00:01:35.568
What resources are capped?

24
00:01:35.568 --> 00:01:39.705
The best reviews connect code structure, tests, and operational signals.

25
00:01:39.705 --> 00:01:48.393
When those three sources agree, teams can change protocol code with more confidence and detect regressions earlier, before unexpected input reaches

26
00:01:48.393 --> 00:01:49.221
production environments.

27
00:01:49.221 --> 00:01:55.840
Review should include parser code, caller expectations, parsed objects, error handling, and operational behavior during maintenance.
